{"author":{"id":"609bf866d4a5d716c19faf5d617b3bf3de478230d29602d72aad5aa90a80466f"},"ops":[{"type":3,"timestamp":1794513481,"nonce":"TRJtFcIm+ZVenD21eEI8HkcLrVA=","message":"Implemented in 2d468084 (local, not pushed): `BytecodeProgram::method_targets` returns a loaded profile's target, else the implementations of the slot among the allocated classes when there are at most two; the emitter's existing guard (9865812c) does the rest. `ASH_AOT_NO_STATIC_DEVIRT` turns it off, as on native.\n\nMeasured on the Mac, one binary, the switch toggled, interleaved pairs, 7 each, medians; wasm32-wasip1-threads, no fibers; the harness is `ash run` for wasmtime and, for Chrome, `ash serve`'s page driven over CDP, reading the page's own \"exited in N ms\". Load was 7.1-8.7 for wasmtime and 6.9-12.9 for Chrome.\n\n    bench_method_call (two subclasses live at one slot)\n      wasmtime   2427 ms -\u003e 955 ms    (0.39)\n      Chrome 157 1757 ms -\u003e 749 ms    (0.43)\n    bench_deltablue\n      wasmtime    202 ms -\u003e 274 ms, best-of 141 vs 140: no difference, the run is about 140 ms and includes the engine compiling the module\n      Chrome      173 ms -\u003e 179 ms, ranges overlap: no difference\n\nBoth programs print the reference checksum with the analysis on and off. Module size: method_call +780 bytes, deltablue +1,699, Pocket Studio (fibers) +3,059 of 15.1 MB. Pocket Studio build CPU is the same within noise.\n\nGates: unit 1069/1069 cases and 10971/10971 assertions on wasm and wasm-fibers with it on; threads pass on wasm-fibers; a new test builds both ways, requires the modules to differ, and requires the interpreter's output from each.\n\nNot confirmed: Linux or the NUC. Both engines here are on aarch64. The mechanism (a direct call in place of call_indirect's table, bounds and signature checks, and a call the engine may inline) is not architecture-specific, but the standing rule is that the NUC says so, so this stays open for that run. deltablue has too little steady